WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.696
Local model serving is not automatically safe just because it runs nearby.

2
00:00:04.696 --> 00:00:12.822
Operators should understand what address the service listens on, who can reach it, and which applications are expected to use it.

3
00:00:12.822 --> 00:00:21.842
A service intended for one user on a local machine has different risk than a service reachable by a team, a lab network, or other internal systems.

4
00:00:21.842 --> 00:00:25.942
Network exposure should be a deliberate architecture decision.

5
00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:36.901
If a model service is reachable by other hosts, teams may need authentication, authorization, firewalling, reverse proxy controls, monitoring, and documented ownership.

6
00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:46.816
The exact controls depend on the environment, but the principle is consistent: do not assume that being internal means access is automatically appropriate.

7
00:00:46.816 --> 00:00:50.021
Data handling is also part of security operations.

8
00:00:50.021 --> 00:01:01.278
Prompts, retrieved context, file contents, and outputs may contain secrets, credentials, personal data, internal plans, customer information, or confidential work product.

9
00:01:01.278 --> 00:01:08.584
Integrations should avoid logging sensitive prompts or outputs unless a governed process explicitly requires it.

10
00:01:08.584 --> 00:01:14.398
When logs are necessary, teams should consider redaction, retention, and who can read them.

11
00:01:14.398 --> 00:01:21.406
Defensive operation focuses on clear access boundaries, privacy-aware logging, and supportable ownership.

12
00:01:21.406 --> 00:01:31.470
Operators should know which workflows are approved, which users or applications can reach the service, what data classes are allowed, and who reviews changes.

13
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:37.210
These choices keep local AI useful without turning it into an unmanaged internal endpoint.
